Title: "From And View"
Id: "bace1f6c-f404-452b-a10b-6fb7772a3607"

Text: "`Enables:` supports build-backed `from:` items and type views in addition to
ordinary capabilities.

```mlg-fragment
Enables:
. from: Y ::= {y__ : ...}
  capability: x_ \"in\" X :<->: x_ member_of Y
. from: P ::= {(p_, q_) : ...}
  as: f(p_) := q_
. view:
  as: S := X is \set
  signifies: S \:represents:/ X
```

A `from:` item describes the structure of a value supplied by a build. It must
contain exactly one of `capability:` or `as:`. A `from:` capability can reduce
facts through the built literal; a `from:`/`as:` binding validates a view from
the source structure to the described callable or value.

`view:` is a zero-argument marker. Its required `as:` section has exactly the
form `<symbol> := <expression> is <type>`. The introduced symbol, expression,
and type are in scope for optional `signifies:`. A view lets the owning
`Declares:`, `Defines:`, or `Realizes:` construct satisfy requirements and
ordinary `\type@value` builds at the view's type. Views are not used to choose
an overloaded operator or a `Disambiguates:` branch.

The removed `Enables: relation:`/`represents:` syntax, `\\coercion`,
`\\encoding`, hard builds (`@!`), and hard type introductions (`is!`) are not
part of the current language."
Id: "efc1a213-388c-4ea9-8b39-3103be5923b5"
